.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-REDEPLOY-APPLICATION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-redeploy-application \- deploy a new build of an application
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap redeploy-application
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
.BR \-\-file | \-f
.I PATH
[\fB\-\-artifact\fR|\fB\-a\fR \fIKEY\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR \fIX.Y.Z\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Deploys a new build of an application from a local file: the jar for a
.IR JAVA
application, the script for
.I PYTHON
or
.IR NODEJS ,
the executable for
.IR BINARY .
.PP
The file is uploaded into the application's own bucket, under the
artifact key the application already uses, and the definition is then
stamped so the manager restarts the running instances onto it - within a
few seconds, since the artifact, the command, the environment and the
credentials a process was handed are all decided when it starts.
.PP
This is
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-file (1)
followed by
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1),
which is exactly how it is implemented - without having to look up which
bucket and key the application uses, and without the failure mode of
running only the first of the two, where the new build sits in the bucket
while every instance goes on running the old one and nothing looks wrong.
.PP
A redeploy has to be a new build, and is refused otherwise:
.IP \(bu 2
the version has to differ from the one deployed. Two deployments under one
version cannot be told apart afterwards - not in a log, not in an
incident, not in
.BR euclid-cli-eap-list-applications (1).
.IP \(bu 2
the artifact has to differ from the one deployed, by MD5. A version bump
carrying identical bytes ships nothing, and the restart it would cause
changes nothing.
.PP
Both are checked here, before the upload, so a refused redeploy leaves the
bucket exactly as it was - and again by the eap module, which is the
boundary. Deploying either on purpose - the same version rebuilt, a
rollback to bytes already seen - is what
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1)
is for.
.PP
An application that is
.I stopped
stays stopped, and comes up on the new build when it is next started.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-application\-id " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the application to deploy. Required.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
The new build, as a local file. Required. Checked before anything is
sent, so a mistyped path costs nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-version " " \fIX.Y.Z\fR
Version this build is. Read out of the local file's own name when not
given - \fIorders\-1.4.0.jar\fR is 1.4.0 - then out of the artifact key,
and required when neither carries one.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-artifact " " \fIKEY\fR
Object key to store the build under, when it should not be the key the
application already uses - a versioned name like
\fIorders\-1.4.jar\fR, say. The application is repointed at it, and the
object under the old key is left in the bucket.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The build was uploaded and the definition stamped. The definition is
printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments,
.B \-\-file
is not a file, the caller is not an authenticated administrator, no
application of that name exists, no version was given and none could be
read from the file or artifact name, the version or the artifact is the
one already deployed, or the upload failed. An upload that
failed leaves the definition untouched, so the application keeps running
on the build it has; if the upload succeeded and only the definition
could not be stamped, the error says which key the build reached and what
to run to deploy it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Deploy a freshly built jar, taking the version from its name:
.RS
.nf
mvn package
euclid-cli eap redeploy-application --application-id file-copy \\
    --file target/file-copy-service-1.4.0.jar
.fi
.RE
.PP
Deploy a build whose file name carries no version:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap redeploy-application --application-id file-copy \\
    --file target/file-copy-service.jar --version 1.4.0
.fi
.RE
.PP
Deploy under a versioned key:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap redeploy-application --application-id orders \\
    --file target/orders-1.4.jar --artifact orders-1.4.jar
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-start-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-file (1)
